 "title": "Idempotency keys",
 "sources": [
  "Stripe API reference, 'Idempotent requests' (official docs, fetched 2026-11-17): idempotency keys let you safely retry requests without performing the same operation twice; the client generates a unique key; for API v1 Stripe saves the status code and body of the first request made with a key and returns the saved response on retries; keys are up to 255 characters and V4 UUIDs are suggested; keys can be removed once they are at least 24 hours old and a reused key then creates a new request; parameters are compared with the original request and an error is returned if they differ, to prevent accidental misuse. https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests",
  "Engineering advice, not from the source: generate the key once per user action and reuse it for every retry of that action. The 'tap pay and the network times out' scene is an illustration, not a quoted incident."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "You tap pay, the network times out, so the app retries, and now you might be charged twice.",
   "caps": "*TAP PAY | NETWORK *TIMES OUT | CHARGED *TWICE?"
  },
  {
   "text": "The fix is an idempotency key: the client makes a unique ID and sends it along with the request.",
   "caps": "*IDEMPOTENCY KEY | A *UNIQUE ID | SENT WITH THE *REQUEST"
  },
  {
   "text": "The server saves the result of the first request under that key.",
   "caps": "SERVER *SAVES | THE FIRST *RESULT"
  },
  {
   "text": "When the retry arrives with the same key, the server doesn't charge again, it just returns the saved response.",
   "caps": "SAME KEY *RETRY | NO *SECOND CHARGE | SAVED *RESPONSE"
  },
  {
   "text": "Stripe suggests random UUIDs, and keys can be up to two hundred fifty five characters.",
   "caps": "SUGGESTED: *UUIDS | UP TO *255 CHARACTERS"
  },
  {
   "text": "And if the same key comes back with different parameters, Stripe returns an error.",
   "caps": "SAME KEY | *DIFFERENT PARAMETERS | = *ERROR"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: keys can be removed after twenty four hours, so a very late retry counts as new.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | KEYS EXPIRE AFTER *24 HOURS | LATE RETRY IS *NEW"
  },
  {
   "text": "So for any write that must not happen twice, send a key, and make it once per action, not once per attempt.",
   "caps": "*SEND A KEY | ONCE PER *ACTION | NOT PER *ATTEMPT"
  }
 ],
